s that I'd like to share  today and one is but you need to get  yourself a sandbox a place to play  around in and this is like what I call  my tinkerer space and whether this is  something that you do at home on the  weekends in your spare time which is not  a healthy work-life balance that I like  to promote as an are ladies organizer or  if your organization is very cool and  and supports you in this mission maybe  you can get a server sandbox from them  either way like taking the initiative to  ask for this thing so that you can learn  the ropes of Linux and DevOps practice  with open source tools and then build  out these proof of concepts to develop a  sense for yourself of what the best  practices in this area are those are all  important things that will then lead you  to being able to support your IT  department in creating a secure analytic  infrastructure for your entire team so  at our studio I have kind of seen my job  is twofold I I really like to promote  best practices and doing smart things  with the tools that we have our  professional products but I also really  like to say you know like  if you're motivated by practical need  and burning curiosity tinkering is great  with one caveat and that's that's what I  alluded to in the previous slide where  we talk about developing a sense for  best practices so when you have a  sandbox you can do a lot of cool stuff  with it  and I've learned over time that there  are things that you can do and there are  things that you should do and those  aren't the same all the time so today I  would have run through like a tinkering  project that I did last summer for use  are and I want to talk about a couple of  cool cloud technologies involving  serverless and R but just bear in mind  that that I am not telling you that this  is a best practice so I want to talk  about plumber API switcher is a really  cool package package plumber developed  by a Jeff Allen who works on the our  studio konnekt team and he gave a  webinar about plumber back in July it's  now available on demand on our website  so do check it out if you're interested  maura just the other day yesterday or  something tweeted this she doesn't even  remember  and it just shows that people are  getting excited about using plumber  api's and and this person in particular  wrote our really according to Martha and  well-explained guide to using docker to  deploy plumber 8 the ice and and this  code on the side kind of shows you the  concept here you're basically getting  from very little essentially just adding  declarations to your code turning  functions that you've written in R into  rest api's the catch is you need  somewhere to serve these api's in order  for anyone outside of you working with  curl on your local machine to use them  at all so I've seen a lot of good  examples like the one I just threw up  the mark tweeted about people who've  used docker to serve these plumber api's  but I thought it would be cool to take  it another step further and use docker  in a server list  cloud computing environment if you  haven't heard of server list before it's  a little bit of a confusing term it's  not magic we're not like doing things  without servers it's basically a term  that said that means you're gonna hand  off the responsibility of managing this  infrastructure to someone else be it  Amazon or adjure or Google cloud and the  particular server list application  platform that I'm interested in using  for this project is Google App Engine  the Google App Engine came out in 2008  and I was in college at the time and I  was like this looks awesome  what does it do I don't know and I never  figured it out ten years later I read a  press release for them that they had now  had custom runtimes so I thought  thinking to myself hey I wonder if I can  put R in there again sandbox tinkering  not best practice necessarily I think  one of so the the basic idea is is that  you bring your code are otherwise and  you have Google cloud platform manage  all the infrastructure for you  and then theoretically you just pay for  what you use as I said R is not included  as a runtime by default they have this  popular crowd of runtimes that they  support are it's not one of them but  with the new custom runtimes you can  bring whatever you want inside of a  docker container so that's what I wanted  to set up I have two little app engines  here and one has plumber inside of  docker and it's serving this REST API  and then because I wanted to document it  and give people like a place to go to  learn about my rest endpoints I also  served up static HTML generated  mark town that was then being served by  nginx  oh I want to pause here to say I do I do  talk to a lot of people in in my  position who get very excited about  docker and I think it's an awesome very  cool technology and my opinion is that  it is awesome and cool when used in the  right situations and so you don't always  need to docker all the things but in  this particular case we do because  that's what I requires so the first part  of this project is that you create a  Google cloud project so you go to the  GCP console you sign it and you create  an account and then you create a project  you should also at this point  download install the Google Cloud SDK so  that you can view things from your  console you don't have to be doing it  from their console there are basically  three parts to a little Google App  Engine you need your docker file you  need your app demo file and this is  literally the entire contents of the app  demo file runtime custom environment  flex that's all you need and then you  need your assets this would be like the  plumber our script or the HTML generated  from our mark town those things and this  is the docker prep that I did for the  plumber app this is just reading in jeff  allen's plumber a docker image that he  has hosted for free on docker hub and  then what I've done is install an extra  package in there ggplot2 because my REST  API produces beautiful plots of iris  data so that people in Python can use  them and then I copy over the plumber  our file into the docker container and  then the final process here is just  exposing port 8080 because that's what  Google App Engine wants to listen on the  doctor prep for nginx is really super  simple as well I just read in the  official repository for nginx docker  image and then essentially the only  thing I do after that is add the static  content  so then we're basically at the final  step here you prepped your doctor files  created this app dot yamo file  gathered all of your our assets together  final two stages are running these two  commands g-cloud app create and g-cloud  app deploy it's at this stage that I  usually forget that I haven't attached  my billing account and like David Smith  was just saying a lot of these cloud  providers provide like free credit to  use and to tinker with  so he said adjure provides $200 GCP  provides $300 and you do have to usually  give them your own credit card  information when you attach these  accounts and that can be very scary but  you do get through GCP a lot of good  visibility into what's burning down this  free credit and how fast it's  disappearing so you can say boost up  burn it all down so that you can stop  getting charged if you need to be I also  want to mention that Google App engines  are not an instantaneous deployment  process by any means leave yourself at  least 15 minutes at least to get your  app up and running on Google cloud if  you need something with fast push button  deployment I work for our studio we have  products just getting started so once  you have this app running a serving  arrest API there are some other fun  things that you can do to prove to  yourself that this is running and  functional and and doing what you expect  it to in a serverless environment so  what I recommend is something really fun  so you can create these siege servers  using the siege utility and you can say  like I want this number of users I want  it like hit this API for this number of  seconds and you can add delay but  literally every time I run one of these  I keep I can't help in my mind like  yelling sea  it's just a lot of fun you can see it  running in real time and they're like  waiting to see and then you can see how  many failures or successes there were  hitting your API like I said I did this  original project for use are over the  summer so I have been tinkering with  extensions to it once you get them into  exploring these managed services that  these cloud providers have available  you'll learn that there are like sloughs  of them and they all do very interesting  different and and some of them are in  beta and some of them die off but but  this one in particular has caught my eye  recently this Google Cloud Composer  because it is a managed Apache airflow  service you haven't heard of Apache  airflow it's basically like if you were  to set up cron but get all of the  information you actually wanted to about  job scheduling so here's what I'd like  to build next and in my next sandbox I'd  like to explore this Google Cloud  Composer using Apache airflow so that I  can kind of track like a workflow in are  running off of REST API endpoints so  this would kind of be like a multi-step  process for training a model or running  like a series of our scripts on a  schedule that you wanted high visibility  into so you want to know like what's  running now how long is it taking I want  a visual diagram of where my job is over  time I want to know if it fails I'm  gonna get an email I want to know if  something suddenly starts running very  slow and all of this can be accomplished  through Apache airflow which i think is  really cool so this is my next project  it just so happens that it also runs  inside of a Google App Engine so as you  can see I'm slowly building up these  skills learning all of these managed  services f